spec:
  grid: odd square, 9-13 rows/cols [observed]
  background: 7 [observed]; dominant color
  foreground: single non-bg color shared by all bars
  bars:
    count: 5-10, scaling with grid size (~0.06 * grid_area)
    shape: 1-cell-thick solid horizontal or vertical segment, odd length 1-9
    length_distribution: mostly 3 (56%), some 5 (26%), occasional 1/7/9; max <= grid_side
    mix: each input has both H and V bars (plus optional length-1) [observed]
  placement_constraints:
    no_overlap_input: bars do not share any pixel
    min_manhattan_gap_input: 2 between any two bars (no 4-adjacency) [observed]
    bars_may_touch_grid_edges: yes
  density: 18-24% foreground pixels [observed]
  transform: rotate each bar 90 degrees around its center (H->V, V->H, length-1 unchanged)
  output_constraints:
    all rotated bars stay within grid bounds (hard, solver asserts)
    no two rotated bars share a pixel (hard, solver asserts)
    rotated bars MAY be 4-adjacent to each other

generation_strategy:
  approach: both-orientations-valid placement
  steps:
    1. Pick odd grid side N in [9,11,13], fill NxN with bg=7, pick one fg color != 7
    2. Decide bar count K in [5, ceil(N*N*0.06)]; pick K odd lengths from {1,3,5,7,...} <= N
    3. For each bar, pick an INPUT orientation (H or V). The center (r,c) must
       satisfy both orientations fitting in-bounds (half = L//2; need
       r-half >= 0, r+half < N, c-half >= 0, c+half < N). This is equivalent
       to requiring center in [half, N-1-half] for both row and column.
    4. Place bars one at a time with greedy random center selection. After
       placing each bar, check that NEITHER its input pixels NOR its rotated
       (output) pixels overlap with any already-placed bar (input or output).
       Reject center on overlap; retry with a new random center.
    5. After all bars placed, render the INPUT grid (bars in their input
       orientation on bg). Output is guaranteed valid by construction.
```
